{-
   `agree` - Design-Test.md §8.

   The assertion with no expected value in it, tested the only way it can be: through the predicate
   rather than through the assertion. `agree` *records* what it finds, so a fixture written in terms
   of it could only assert the cases that agree - and the whole of what this file is about is what
   the report says when they do not. `disagreementOf` is public for exactly this reason and the
   library says so where it is declared.

   What is asserted here is the report's *text*, which is unusual for a corpus that mostly asserts
   numbers. It is the point of the feature: "three ways, two agreed - `js` answered 17, `native` and
   `native-noopt` answered 16" is a sentence somebody acts on, and a version of it that named the
   majority first, or that said "agreement failed", would pass every structural check and be worth
   nothing.
-}


-- Two ways that agree is the ordinary case and costs one comparison. Nothing is recorded, and the
-- assertion below is that nothing was.
@test fn waysThatAgree() -> {}:
    let ways = [Way {name: "direct", run: () -> "16"},
                Way {name: "folded", run: () -> "{8 + 8}"}]

    checkNothing(disagreementOf("total", ways))
    agree("total", ways)

{-
   The odd one out, named first.

   Three ways and two answers, which is §8's own example. The minority group leads the sentence
   because that is what is being looked for; a report that put the two agreeing ways first would
   bury the answer in the middle of the line.
-}
@test fn theOddOneOutIsNamedFirst() -> {}:
    let ways = [Way {name: "native", run: () -> "16"},
                Way {name: "js", run: () -> "17"},
                Way {name: "native-noopt", run: () -> "16"}]

    match disagreementOf("total", ways):
        Nothing -> check(False, "three ways with two answers must not agree")
        Just(said) -> checkEqual(said, "total: 3 ways, 2 agreed - `js` answered 17 - `native` and `native-noopt` answered 16")

-- Three ways and three answers: every group is of one, and the order is the order they were written
-- in. What this pins is that a run with no majority still reports every way rather than picking one.
@test fn everyWayDisagreeing() -> {}:
    let ways = [Way {name: "a", run: () -> "1"},
                Way {name: "b", run: () -> "2"},
                Way {name: "c", run: () -> "3"}]

    match disagreementOf("count", ways):
        Nothing -> check(False, "three answers must not agree")
        Just(said) -> checkEqual(said, "count: 3 ways, 1 agreed - `a` answered 1 - `b` answered 2 - `c` answered 3")

{-
   Fewer than two ways is a failure and not a vacuous pass.

   This is `test/README.md`'s empty-corpus guard in the place it is easiest to trip over: a matrix
   that produced one build, or a list an `if` emptied, is an `agree` that holds because there is
   nothing for it to disagree with. One way agreeing with itself asserts nothing, and something that
   asserts nothing must not be mistaken for something that asserts everything.
-}
@test fn oneWayIsNotAgreement() -> {}:
    checkJust(disagreementOf("alone", [Way {name: "only", run: () -> "1"}]))
    checkJust(disagreementOf("none", [] :: [Way]))

-- The mood that ends the test. `requireAgree` records *and* answers `Err`, so `?` is an early
-- return - which is what a precondition wants and what the four claims below would otherwise be
-- noise after.
@test fn agreementAsAPrecondition() -> Test:
    let ways = [Way {name: "left", run: () -> "same"}, Way {name: "right", run: () -> "same"}]
    requireAgree("shape", ways)?
    checkEqual(1 + 1, 2)
    return Ok({})
